            "Three real requirements, each with the wrong and right way to handle the platform difference.\n\n**1. A date picker.**\n- *Wrong*: render a custom JS calendar and hope it matches each platform's conventions.\n- *Right*: use the platform's own picker (`@react-native-community/datetimepicker` shows the iOS wheel and the Android dialog natively). Native means *their* conventions, including the ones users' muscle memory expects. Style around it; do not replace it.\n\n**2. Back navigation.**\n- *Wrong*: add a custom back button everywhere and handle `hardwareBackPress` yourself, inconsistently.\n- *Right*: use a navigation library that already wires the iOS swipe-back gesture, the Android hardware back button, and header back arrows to one stack. Platform behaviour is a solved problem at the navigation layer — the lesson on navigation takes it from there.\n\n**3. A backup indicator.**\n- *Wrong*: `if (Platform.OS === \"ios\") showICloudLabel() else showGoogleLabel()` scattered through six components.\n- *Right*: one platform-specific module (`backup.tsx` exporting `BackupProviderLabel`), with `backup.ios.tsx` and `backup.android.tsx` implementing the same interface. The branch exists **once**, at the edge, and the rest of the app is platform-blind — which is what makes \"it works on both\" a claim you can test rather than hope for.\n\nThe pattern behind all three: **platform differences are integration concerns.** Keep them at the boundary — native pickers, navigation libraries, platform files — and let the interior of your app speak one language. When a difference must reach business logic, isolate it behind a shared function with two implementations and one test suite that runs against both, so \"works on iOS\" and \"works on Android\" become checkable statements rather than vibes.",
