          kind: "warning",
          body:
            "- **Expecting the DOM.** No `document`, no `window`, no `className`, no HTML. A web package that imports them will crash at runtime — check a library's React Native support before adopting it.\n- **Web CSS habits.** There is no cascade, no selector specificity, no inheritance, no external stylesheet. Arrays of style objects with later-wins order are the whole system.\n- **Forgetting the default is `column`.** Rows need `flexDirection: \"row\"` explicitly — the mirror of the web's habit.\n- **Pixel thinking.** `width: 300` is 300 *density-independent* points, not device pixels — that is deliberate (same physical size across densities). Design against that, and never hardcode a screen's pixel dimensions.\n- **Putting text outside `<Text>`.** A `View` containing raw strings throws. Everything readable lives in `Text`.\n- **Chatty bridge calls in a loop.** Thousands of per-item synchronous calls across the boundary stutter. Batch them, move work native-side, or use a list component that renders lazily.\n- **Ignoring platform conventions.** Custom everything where the platform has an answer (pickers, back gestures, tab bars, switches) makes an app feel foreign on *both* platforms.\n- **One runtime's debugging habits.** There are no element inspectors; use the JS debugger, component inspector, network logs and native logs (Xcode/Android Studio) — and remember logs may come from two worlds.\n- **Assuming every browser API exists.** No `localStorage`, no `alert`, no CSS media queries, no arbitrary timers in a suspended app. Each has a React Native counterpart you must adopt deliberately.\n- **Sharing code too early with a monorepo of native modules.** Start with one app, extract shared *logic* (types, validation, API client) when a second consumer exists — the same evidence-based rule as component extraction.",
